From 06c0b7b84842cba5556d2a5319e0cd744aec4f61 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Trevor Wu <trevor.wu@mediatek.com>
Date: Thu, 3 Sep 2026 17:45:08 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] NVIDIA: SAUCE: ASoC: mediatek: mt8901: add system sleep
 support

BugLink: https://bugs.launchpad.net/bugs/2166301

Add system sleep suspend/resume callbacks to the mt8901 ASoC platform
driver so the audio block releases its clock and MTCMOS power requests
across suspend and the platform can reach its deepest sleep state. The
SSPM firmware reference counts power domain requests, so per-module
requests are intentionally symmetric with the other operating system
supported on this platform.

On resume, restore the AFE hardware state: reprogram the memory
interfaces, interconnect routing and interrupt state so streams can
restart after system suspend without reloading the driver. Partial
failures during either transition are unwound instead of resuming into
a half-suspended configuration.

Probe establishes the runtime PM baseline before publishing the
component. Its initial put may leave the device active when another
usage reference is outstanding (user space forbidding runtime PM before
bind); the bypass resume only brought up MTCMOS and the register clock
and no later runtime get calls runtime_resume() on an active device, so
probe then brings up the rest of the datapath (main clock, APLL gates,
DDR request) itself, leaving the device in the state a full
runtime_resume() produces. On unbind a devres action, ordered after the
ASoC component and the interrupt are released and before runtime PM is
disabled, forces a runtime suspend so whatever probe or a runtime resume
left held is released regardless of that reference, with no users left
to touch powered-down hardware, and the PM status matches the hardware
for the next bind.

Signed-off-by: Trevor Wu <trevor.wu@mediatek.com>
Co-developed-by: David Cemin <dcemin@nvidia.com>
Signed-off-by: David Cemin <dcemin@nvidia.com>
Acked-by: Jamie Nguyen <jamien@nvidia.com>
Acked-by: Carol L Soto <csoto@nvidia.com>
Signed-off-by: Brad Figg <bfigg@nvidia.com>
---
